{
  "abstract": "We fix all three level primes in the shorter Alpöge–Claude construction across blocks of cutoffs and eligible quadratic fields of discriminant at most $2D$, with $D=o(\\log X)$. A smooth divisor mesh fixes the target weights and one completed determinant ideal per field and rank. A putative zeta zero to the right of the critical line gives common sample weights with a large signed fourth-power sum outside the zero-density exceptional set. In each sufficiently large dyadic field window we construct $D^{1-o(1)}$ disjoint pairs with complementary quadratic signs through $c\\sqrt{\\log D}$. Localized genus terms cancel. The unitary mass and Weyl dimensions give an integral product of completed ideals whose normalized degree preserves the paired criterion to arbitrary inverse-power accuracy. At the prescribed critical point of the last lift, matching twists through $Y$ yields an oriented Rallis determinant ratio with normalized log denominator at least $(R/2)\\log Y-O(1)$ away from both conductors. For each completed basis all menu ideals divide a fixed cleared untwisted Gram. Terminal denominators can grow away from its entire support. At the reference primes a harmonic depth average bounds the capped rank-one loss, and cyclic layer products give the signed higher-rank test. On sufficiently slowly growing field windows, a conductor-616 construction selects common old primes for which cyclic CM induction occurs in the original good space at every occupied rank. The induced part has exponentially small dimension share. Exact reduced-rank examples give forward valuations $-19/20$ at $5$ for an order-$25$ CM elliptic twist and $-2$ at $2$ for a quadratic rank-two twist with both split Euler factors retained. The paired odd-rank lower bound remains open. The released period comparisons and the bounded integral vacuum of Paper 215 v2 supply the paired estimate and the stated conditional deduction of ordinary RH.",
